</section>		<section id=3D"content-body">
<p>API attacks are on the rise, opening organizations to new security risks=
.</p>=20
<p>Traditional website-based cyberattacks target the application itself, bu=
t as <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/searchapparchitecture/Guide-to-b=
uilding-an-enterprise-API-strategy">companies expose a growing number of we=
bsite APIs</a> to the internet, these connections are increasingly drawing =
the attention of threat actors.</p>
<div class=3D"ad-wrapper ad-embedded">=20
 <div id=3D"halfpage" class=3D"ad ad-hp">
 =20
 </div>=20
 <div id=3D"mu-1" class=3D"ad ad-mu">
 =20
 </div>=20
</div>=20
<p>An <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/searchapparchitecture/definitio=
n/application-program-interface-API">API</a> lets software communicate with=
 other software to share data and functionality. For example, an API might =
let a user log in to an application with credentials from a third-party acc=
ount, such as Facebook or Google.</p>=20
<p>An <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/tip/API-keys-Wea=
knesses-and-security-best-practices">API key</a> authorizes a connection be=
tween software programs, acting like a password to identify the application=
 calling the API and prove it has access permissions.</p>=20
<p>API requests are similar to the access requests users send from their br=
owsers, meaning many attacks that work against websites can compromise APIs=
 as well.</p>=20
<section class=3D"section main-article-chapter" data-menu-title=3D"5 types =
of API attacks">
 <h2 class=3D"section-title"><i class=3D"icon" data-icon=3D"1"></i>5 types =
of API attacks</h2>
 <p>Malicious hackers employ a number of API attacks and API abuse techniqu=
es. These include the following.</p>
 <h3>1. Injection attacks</h3>
 <p>An API is vulnerable to <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/searchsec=
urity/feature/How-to-prevent-SQL-injection-with-prepared-statements">SQL in=
jection</a> or cross-site scripting (<a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/=
searchsecurity/definition/cross-site-scripting">XSS</a>) if developers don'=
t properly sanitize its query parameters to ensure the API sends and receiv=
es only appropriate data.</p>
 <p>In injection attacks, cybercriminals insert malicious code into their q=
ueries to trick the API into granting unauthorized access to sensitive data=
. They can then steal, modify, delete and replace that information as they =
choose.</p>
 <figure class=3D"main-article-image full-col" data-img-fullsize=3D"https:/=
/www.techtarget.com/rms/onlineimages/security_incidents_from_insecure_apis-=
f.png">
  <img data-src=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/rms/onlineimages/security_inc=
idents_from_insecure_apis-f_mobile.png" class=3D"lazy" data-srcset=3D"https=
://www.techtarget.com/rms/onlineimages/security_incidents_from_insecure_api=
s-f_mobile.png 960w,https://www.techtarget.com/rms/onlineimages/security_in=
cidents_from_insecure_apis-f.png 1280w" alt=3D"Chart showing 92% of organiz=
ations surveyed experienced API-related security incidents in the previous =
12 months" height=3D"286" width=3D"560">
  <figcaption>
   <i class=3D"icon pictures" data-icon=3D"z"></i>In 2023, Enterprise Strat=
egy Group found the vast majority of organizations had recently experienced=
 cybersecurity incidents related to insecure APIs.
  </figcaption>
  <div class=3D"main-article-image-enlarge">
   <i class=3D"icon" data-icon=3D"w"></i>
  </div>
 </figure>
 <h3>2. Broken access control attacks</h3>
 <p>In some cases, threat actors can bypass standard access control guardra=
ils by interacting with target APIs directly and exploiting their security =
vulnerabilities. At worst, APIs with broken authentication and authorizatio=
n controls set the stage for massive data breaches.</p>
 <p>In a broken object-level authorization attack, for example, an API migh=
t successfully authenticate a user through a userID value in the API reques=
t but accept a different ID in the query parameter.</p>
 <p>If the API does not validate that the authenticated user should have ac=
cess to the requested data -- i.e., that the userID in the request matches =
the userID in the query -- then an attacker can easily access details of an=
y other user's account.</p>
 <p>And if <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/feature/Imp=
lement-API-rate-limiting-to-reduce-attack-surfaces">API rate limiting</a> i=
sn't in use -- restricting the number of calls one user can make in a given=
 time frame to reduce the attack surface -- then the threat actor could exf=
iltrate massive amounts of sensitive information.</p>
 <p>In some past attacks, vulnerable API endpoints did not require any user=
 authentication at all, leaving them wide open to malicious hackers.</p>
 <h3>3. Excessive data exposure</h3>
 <p>Organizations that do not manage their APIs effectively often allow too=
 many APIs -- for example, old or test versions -- to be <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.theserverside.com/blog/Coffee-Talk-Java-News-Stories-and-Opinions/How-=
not-to-spill-the-Tea-A-real-world-app-security-lesson">exposed to the inter=
net</a>. In some cases, such APIs send too much information back when they =
receive requests, potentially exposing sensitive data.</p>
 <h3>4. DoS and DDoS attacks</h3>
 <p>If an API is not protected from <a href=3D"https://www.techtarget.com/s=
earchsecurity/tip/DoS-vs-DDoS-How-they-differ-and-the-damage-they-cause">de=
nial-of-service and distributed denial-of-service attacks</a>, bad actors c=
an launch a brute-force attack, in which they send a huge volume of data to=
 the API and cause the entire website to stop responding.</p>
 <h3>5. Third-party attacks</h3>
 <p>Attackers can also target APIs through third parties. A malicious hacke=
r might be able to compromise a trusted partner that has a legitimate API k=
ey, for example, and use it to access the target API.</p>
